1
00:00:00,442 --> 00:00:08,756
A coding assistant can fill in punctuation, repeat a familiar loop pattern, or choose the algorithm that defines the whole program.

2
00:00:08,866 --> 00:00:12,946
These actions have very different implications for the human's control.

3
00:00:13,056 --> 00:00:19,807
Completing more text is not automatically better assistance if it passes through a decision the human intended to make.

4
00:00:20,084 --> 00:00:25,654
Training LLM Agents to Empower Humans studies this problem with the document as a state.

5
00:00:25,765 --> 00:00:33,066
The assistant proposes text; the human can accept, reject, or finish, and can add their own text.

6
00:00:33,177 --> 00:00:41,567
Effective empowerment measures how the human's next contribution influences the future document, rather than measuring how much control

7
00:00:41,677 --> 00:00:43,395
the assistant accumulates.

8
00:00:43,672 --> 00:00:50,667
The practical method uses offline text to construct training examples that complete predictable continuations.

9
00:00:50,778 --> 00:00:55,166
The ambition is to help the human reach a consequential choice more quickly.

10
00:00:55,276 --> 00:01:00,650
Our animations use an invented token sequence so that you can inspect every probability.

11
00:01:00,761 --> 00:01:06,586
They do not claim to reproduce an actual model's tokenization or its confidence on real code.

12
00:01:07,858 --> 00:01:13,131
The information objective still looks hard to compute over all possible future documents.

13
00:01:13,242 --> 00:01:21,831
Start with a standard identity: mutual information equals the human action entropy minus the uncertainty about that action after the

14
00:01:21,941 --> 00:01:23,226
future is observed.

15
00:01:23,337 --> 00:01:27,830
The second term cannot be negative, so action entropy is an upper bound.

16
00:01:28,106 --> 00:01:30,145
This gives a one-way implication.

17
00:01:30,256 --> 00:01:36,310
If the human's next action is almost certain, it cannot transmit much information through this channel.

18
00:01:36,421 --> 00:01:39,455
But high uncertainty does not prove high empowerment.

19
00:01:39,565 --> 00:01:46,364
A person could make many random edits that are later overwritten, leaving the final document independent of the choice.

20
00:01:46,641 --> 00:01:49,475
That asymmetry is essential to reading the paper.

21
00:01:49,586 --> 00:01:56,055
The method uses low estimated entropy as a signal for text that may be uninteresting for the human to supply.

22
00:01:56,166 --> 00:02:01,360
It does not exactly calculate the mutual information of every proposed completion.

23
00:02:01,471 --> 00:02:09,489
Our lab therefore displays a surprisal threshold and a completion length, rather than mislabeling either as measured human empowerment.

24
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:14,440
Entropy is an expectation over possible human actions.

25
00:02:14,551 --> 00:02:21,779
We do not know the complete human policy, so the method uses a pretrained language model as a likelihood proxy.

26
00:02:21,890 --> 00:02:27,487
For one observed continuation, negative log model probability is a surprisal sample.

27
00:02:27,598 --> 00:02:30,739
It is not the entropy of the whole action distribution.

28
00:02:31,016 --> 00:02:39,188
If samples truly came from the human policy and the predictor matched that policy, averaging their surprisals would recover the entropy.

29
00:02:39,299 --> 00:02:47,310
With a mismatched predictor, the average instead equals cross-entropy: human entropy plus a Kullback Leibler mismatch term.

30
00:02:47,421 --> 00:02:54,297
A surprised model might therefore be seeing an unusual but obvious human action, rather than an important decision.

31
00:02:54,573 --> 00:02:56,959
This does not make the heuristic useless.

32
00:02:57,069 --> 00:02:59,530
It identifies an assumption to test.

33
00:02:59,641 --> 00:03:07,226
Calibration, domain shift, tokenization, and which corpus supplies the samples can all affect the threshold.

34
00:03:07,336 --> 00:03:15,598
In the lab, changing a candidate token's assigned probability moves the stopping point without changing any underlying human capability.

35
00:03:15,709 --> 00:03:19,942
That illustrates why the score should remain labeled as a model-based proxy.

36
00:03:21,213 --> 00:03:25,702
A completion's probability factors into conditional token probabilities.

37
00:03:25,813 --> 00:03:29,650
Taking a negative logarithm converts their product into a sum.

38
00:03:29,761 --> 00:03:36,022
Each additional token adds a nonnegative surprisal cost, so the cumulative curve can only rise.

39
00:03:36,132 --> 00:03:41,934
The method chooses the longest continuation whose total cost stays below a selected threshold.

40
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:46,950
Suppose four predictable tokens have cumulative surprisal point two nats.

41
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:49,897
The next token alone adds one point four nats.

42
00:03:50,008 --> 00:03:55,157
With a threshold of one nat, the assistant's training target ends before that token.

43
00:03:55,268 --> 00:03:59,293
Raising the threshold can admit it and perhaps more tokens afterward.

44
00:03:59,404 --> 00:04:04,589
The stopping rule depends on the sum, not just the least likely individual token.

45
00:04:04,866 --> 00:04:06,884
All logarithms here are natural.

46
00:04:06,994 --> 00:04:14,681
Therefore a surprisal threshold of eta corresponds to a sequence-probability threshold of exponential minus eta.

47
00:04:14,791 --> 00:04:20,731
If you choose base-two logs instead, the corresponding expression uses two to the minus eta.

48
00:04:20,841 --> 00:04:27,593
The numerical lab handles the empty-prefix case explicitly when even the first token crosses the threshold.

49
00:04:28,864 --> 00:04:31,699
The implementation is surprisingly concrete.

50
00:04:31,810 --> 00:04:34,316
Sample a program and choose a prefix.

51
00:04:34,426 --> 00:04:37,394
Score the remaining suffix with the likelihood model.

52
00:04:37,504 --> 00:04:45,836
Walk forward until cumulative surprisal crosses the threshold, then use the preceding continuation as the assistant's training target.

53
00:04:45,947 --> 00:04:50,733
This turns an information-theoretic motivation into a data-selection procedure.

54
00:04:51,009 --> 00:04:54,758
Fine-tune the assistant to produce these selected continuations.

55
00:04:54,869 --> 00:05:02,930
The practical algorithm does not need to enumerate future documents or run an exact mutual-information reinforcement learning loop

56
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:04,666
during target construction.

57
00:05:04,776 --> 00:05:12,424
That distinction is important in a course on empowerment-based reinforcement learning: the theoretical objective can inspire a

58
00:05:12,534 --> 00:05:17,890
supervised training recipe rather than appearing literally as a policy-gradient reward.

59
00:05:18,166 --> 00:05:23,093
The included code implements the stopping calculation on explicit probabilities.

60
00:05:23,204 --> 00:05:28,950
It is a transparent reference for the arithmetic, not a complete language-model training system.

61
00:05:29,061 --> 00:05:37,647
To build the full method, you would additionally need a tokenizer, a likelihood model, a dataset, training configuration, and an

62
00:05:37,758 --> 00:05:42,901
evaluation protocol that measures the human's experience with the resulting assistant.

63
00:05:44,172 --> 00:05:51,705
The authors report an eighteen-person study in which participants preferred their empowerment-trained assistant seventy-eight percent of

64
00:05:51,815 --> 00:05:56,312
the time, alongside evaluations with simulated coding users.

65
00:05:56,423 --> 00:05:59,527
These findings support the method in the studied settings.

66
00:05:59,638 --> 00:06:07,324
They do not establish that shorter completions, or any fixed surprisal threshold, will be best for every user and task.

67
00:06:07,601 --> 00:06:16,830
For a new deployment, evaluate preference, acceptance, task outcomes, and whether the assistant leaves the intended decisions to the

68
00:06:16,941 --> 00:06:17,495
human.

69
00:06:17,606 --> 00:06:24,084
Inspect failures where model uncertainty reflects unfamiliar vocabulary rather than a genuine choice.

70
00:06:24,195 --> 00:06:32,656
Also compare against well-tuned completion-length baselines, because the goal is better assistance, not merely changing the number of

71
00:06:32,766 --> 00:06:34,094
generated tokens.

72
00:06:34,371 --> 00:06:39,450
Across the series, the same information-theoretic lens appears in several roles.

73
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:41,827
DIAYN learns recognizable skills.

74
00:06:41,938 --> 00:06:45,409
Geometry and SEE ask where many skills remain possible.

75
00:06:45,520 --> 00:06:49,378
Representation learning asks which features matter for control.

76
00:06:49,489 --> 00:06:52,655
Assistance changes whose influence is optimized.

77
00:06:52,766 --> 00:06:59,171
The definitions, assumptions, and measurement channel determine what each objective actually promises.
